Thouret's committee proposed that the title of **active citizen** (those qualifying to vote in the primary assemblies of their canton) be granted to individuals who met the following five qualifications:

1.  To be French or to have become French;
2.  To have reached one's majority (the age was set at 25);
3.  To have resided in the canton for at least one year;
4.  To pay direct taxes at a rate equal to the local value of three days of work;
5.  To not be a servant (due to personal relationships deemed incompatible with the independence necessary to exercise political rights).
